<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antigonus III Doson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 263–<a href="/years/221-bce">221 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> III Doson was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> from 229 BCE. Called in by the Achaean League against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, he took cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a>, including Orchomenus and Mantinea in 223 BCE, and defeated Cleomenes at Sellasia in 222 BCE. He organised a Hellenic League under Macedonian leadership.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson.jpg" alt="Antigonus III Doson" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: uploader - Bjankuloski06 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antigonus_Doson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antigonus III Doson belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid dynasty</a> through his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-the-fair">Demetrius the Fair</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a> who was briefly king of Cyrene. He was a half-cousin of his predecessor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-aetolicus">Demetrius II Aetolicus</a>. According to Eusebius his mother was a Thessalian noblewoman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>, daughter of Pauliclitus of Larissa. Demetrius the Fair died around 250 BCE, assassinated at the instigation of his young bride <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berenice-ii">Berenice II</a> after he became the lover of her mother, Apama II. The by-name Doson means &quot;going to give&quot;; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> it implied a man given to promising but not performing, though its exact meaning is unclear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Demetrius II died in battle in 229 BCE his son, the later Philip V, was nine years old. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> the Macedonian army and nobility thought the situation too unstable to wait for him, and turned to Doson, who became regent and married Demetrius's widow Chryseis, Philip's mother. Only after he defeated the Dardanii invaders and put down a rebellion by the Thessalians, and showed a moderate rule, was he given the title of king. Even as king he apparently saw himself as a caretaker for Philip.</p><p>In under a decade he secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>'s borders and restored its position as the dominant power in the region. He formed alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and the Achaean League rather than seeking direct rule over his neighbours. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> tried to establish control over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus of Sicyon</a> invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> to intervene in 226 BCE. He based himself on the heights above Corinth and revived the Hellenic League of Philip II under his own leadership, probably in 224 BCE, calling it the League of Leagues. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sellasia">Battle of Sellasia</a> in 222 BCE the Spartan forces were overwhelmed, and Cleomenes escaped with a few horsemen and took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Antigonus kept his soldiers from plundering Sparta, saying his enemy was Cleomenes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>While <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> was campaigning in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> invaded Macedonia from the north. On his way back he passed through Tegea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, where he was honoured by the Achaean League and other cities at the opening of the Nemean Games. He then fought the Illyrians and won, but fell ill during the battle, possibly from a ruptured blood vessel, and died in 221 BCE, soon after returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antigonus III Doson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…at night attacks and surprises such as the capture of Acrocorinth, but the league suffered a series of defeats at the hands of Cleomenes III, at Mount Lykaion, Ladokeia and the Hekatombaion near Dyme in 227–226 BCE, which forced Aratus to call in <b>Antigonus III Doson of Macedon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Antigonus III Doson</b> crushed Cleomenes III of Sparta at the Battle of Sellasia in 222 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His League of Corinth, uniting the major city-states except Sparta under a Macedonian hegemon for a war presented as punishment of the Persians and the liberation of the Greek cities of Asia Minor, was the model <b>Antigonus III Doson</b> revived a century later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The reforming kings Agis IV and Cleomenes III tried to rebuild the citizen body by cancelling debts and redistributing land, but Cleomenes was defeated at Sellasia in 222 BCE by <b>Antigonus III Doson</b> and the Achaean League.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After it joined Cleomenes III of Sparta, the Achaean League and <b>Antigonus Doson</b> destroyed it in 223 BCE and renamed it Antigoneia.</blockquote><a href="/state/mantinea">Mantinea</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Hellenistic period Orchomenus was taken in turn by the Aetolian League, Sparta and the Achaean League, and <b>Antigonus Doson</b> of the Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia captured it in 223 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/orchomenus-arcadia">Orchomenus (Arcadia)</a></li></ul></section>
